\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
Let $X$ be a finite connected three-dimensional CW complex and $\widetilde X$ its universal cover. If $H_i(\widetilde X;\mathbb Z)=0$ for $i>2$, and $H^3(X;M)=0$ for every $\mathbb Z[\pi_1X]$-module $M$, must $X$ be homotopy equivalent to a finite CW complex of dimension at most two?

\subsection{Short English statement}
Let $X$ be a finite connected three-dimensional CW complex and $\widetilde X$ its universal cover. If $H_i(\widetilde X;\mathbb Z)=0$ for $i>2$, and $H^3(X;M)=0$ for every $\mathbb Z[\pi_1X]$-module $M$, must $X$ be homotopy equivalent to a finite CW complex of dimension at most two?

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{itemize}
\item[S1] Original question source or mathematical context.
\url{https://www.math.utah.edu/~bestvina/eprints/questions-updated.pdf}.
\item[S2] OpenAI, manuscript citation page. Source of the formulation motivating this local problem.
\url{https://github.com/openai/math/blob/adc7f1241b42e322a6451854ab7e4b4c146bf78a/preprints/A-Counterexample-to-Walls-D2-Problem-October-6-2026/README.md}.
\end{itemize}

